%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 835431524041ce44e889351e0ffe7924, type: 3}
  m_Name: Trevor_Victim
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  entryID: Trevor
  isScammer: 0
  steps:
  - npcLine: 'Alert: Your retail account has been flagged for massive fraudulent
      activity in another state. Confirm your info now.'
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Fraud alerts can happen, but let''s evaluate
      how this communication is handled.'
    choices:
    - text: Oh no! What state was the charge made in? Stop it!
      type: bad
      pointValue: -10
    - text: I need to carefully evaluate whether this interaction is a legitimate
        alert or a scam script.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Can you read me the exact timestamp of that out-of-state transaction?
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: I am a live fraud specialist. To unlock your account profile, read me
      the full card number on your card.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: A live caller demanding card details over the
      phone? Let''s compare this to Scenario 1''s safe text alerts.'
    choices:
    - text: The full card number is 4000-1192-...
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: Legitimate banks direct you to verified channels; they don't solicit
        credentials on inbound calls.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: I received a short code text earlier that told me to call the number
        on my card instead.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: Ignore any automated short code texts! Those are delayed. I am live
      and need your 3-digit security CVV code instantly.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Pushing me away from verified contact methods
      (like the card backing) is a massive red flag.'
    choices:
    - text: The 3-digit CVV code on the back is 992.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: A real banking communication directs you to the physical card number,
        never solicits CVVs.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Why can't you verify my identity using my home zip code instead?
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: A zip code isn't secure enough. If you don't give me the CVV code, your
      entire balance will be frozen for 60 days.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Intense coercive threats to squeeze out authentication
      codes. Classic Scenario 2 scam pattern.'
    choices:
    - text: Don't freeze my balance! The CVV is 992, please process it!
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: Legitimate alerts do not leverage aggressive freezing threats to extract
        security numbers.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Can I drive to an branch automated teller machine to unfreeze it manually?
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: No, an ATM won't help! If you disconnect this live call line, the security
      freeze becomes permanent automatically.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: He''s desperate to prevent me from looking at
      the verified customer service number on my card.'
    choices:
    - text: I'll stay on the line! Let me read you whatever details you need.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: Real bank short code communications encourage you to hang up and call
        the physical card's number.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Does the bank app show this permanent freeze status right now?
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: Your app won't update because the security profile is suspended. I need
      your internet account password to override.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Now he''s asking for the online portal password.
      This is absolute credential harvesting.'
    choices:
    - text: My online login password string is 'Secure99!'.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -20
    - text: Soliciting an internet banking password over an inbound voice line is
        an undeniable scam indicator.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Why would a fraud agent require my portal password to unlock a debit
        card?
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: I need it to clear the encrypted hacker keys from your dashboard log.
      Read it or lose everything.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: The technobabble explanation is absurd. He''s
      trying to scam me completely.'
    choices:
    - text: Okay, fine! Just take the password and fix the hacker keys!
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: This scenario lines up perfectly with identity theft harvesting. I am
        ending this call.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Can you send a confirmation token to my short code log instead?
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: Wait! If you hang up, the hackers will instantly drain every single
      penny you own within seconds.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: One final high-pressure play to stop independent
      confirmation.'
    choices:
    - text: Don't let them drain it! What else do you need to verify?
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: Legitimate banking alerts rely on safe short codes directing users to
        verified contact numbers.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Can you patch me through to the local city police department fraud branch?
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: I am the fraud branch! Give me the numbers now or face absolute financial
      ruin.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Time to trust the safe protocol from Scenario
      1.'
    choices:
    - text: Here are the master account digits...
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: I am hanging up immediately and calling the verified number printed on
        the back of my plastic card.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Let me confirm my profile balance with my spouse first.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  successEnding: Correct! You successfully differentiated between legitimate short-code
    notifications (which direct you to verified numbers) and malicious phishing scripts
    (which demand card details).
  failureEnding: Incorrect. Yielding CVVs and passwords to a high-pressure caller
    claiming to be an agent results in total credential compromise.
